# term: (display, meaning — one line, when it comes up — one line)
G = {}
G["stats"] = [
 ("Clustered standard errors","Error bars that account for users being measured many times, instead of pretending every row is a fresh person.","The moment your unit of randomisation is the user but your rows are sessions — ignore it and every result looks significant."),
 ("A/A test","Run the experiment with both arms identical. Any 'winner' you see is your false-positive rate showing up.","Asked when you claim your experiment platform works. If A/A wins 20% of the time, your A/B results mean nothing."),
 ("OEC","Overall Evaluation Criterion — the single metric you agreed to judge the test on, chosen before launch.","When you have eight metrics and three moved. Without a pre-named OEC you are picking a winner after the fact."),
 ("Design effect","How much a cluster or ratio metric inflates your variance versus simple random sampling.","Sample-size maths for any metric measured per-session or per-impression; skip it and you under-power the test."),
 ("Statistical power","The chance your test detects a real effect of a given size. Usually targeted at 80%.","Before launch, paired with MDE. An underpowered test that shows nothing has told you nothing."),
 ("Minimum detectable effect","The smallest lift the test could reliably catch at your sample size.","Paired with power. 'We saw no difference' is only meaningful next to the MDE you could have seen."),
 ("Benjamini-Hochberg","A correction that controls the share of false discoveries among your wins, rather than the chance of any error at all.","Twenty guardrail metrics. Bonferroni is too harsh; BH keeps power while bounding the false-discovery rate."),
 ("Max-t correction","A multiple-comparison fix that uses the correlation between your metrics instead of assuming independence.","When your metrics move together — Bonferroni over-corrects badly in that case."),
 ("Alpha spending","Budgeting your false-positive allowance across several planned peeks at the data.","How to look early without the peeking problem — you decide the schedule up front."),
 ("Pre-registration","Writing the hypothesis, metric and stopping rule down before seeing any data.","The answer to 'how do we stop ourselves rationalising the result afterwards'."),
 ("Wilson interval","A confidence interval for a proportion that behaves sanely at tiny counts and near 0% or 100%.","0 conversions out of 40. The textbook normal interval gives you nonsense; Wilson does not."),
 ("Credible interval","The Bayesian interval — the range the parameter is actually in with 95% probability, given your prior.","When someone asks what a confidence interval means and you want the answer that matches their intuition."),
 ("Delta method","A way to get error bars for a ratio or other transformed quantity from the variance of its parts.","Any per-user ratio metric — click-through, revenue-per-session. The naive standard error is wrong."),
 ("Type M error","Getting the direction right but the magnitude badly inflated.","Why underpowered studies that 'replicate' still mislead — the effect is real, the size is fantasy."),
 ("Winsorising","Clipping extreme values to a percentile instead of deleting them.","Revenue metrics with one whale. Keeps the row, kills the leverage."),
 ("Smooth bootstrap","Bootstrap that adds small noise when resampling, so the resampled distribution is not purely discrete.","Bootstrapping a quantile on sparse data, where the plain bootstrap gets stuck on a few values."),
 ("Empirical-Bayes shrinkage","Pulling small-sample estimates toward the overall average, by an amount the data itself decides.","Ranking items where some have three ratings and some have three thousand."),
 ("Beta-binomial","A binomial whose rate varies across the population, rather than being one fixed number.","Conversion rates that differ per user — the plain binomial underestimates variance."),
 ("Bradley-Terry","A model that turns pairwise win/loss records into a single quality score per item.","Scoring models from head-to-head preference data, including LLM arena rankings."),
 ("Variance reduction","Any technique that shrinks noise without biasing the estimate, so you need fewer users.","CUPED is the common one. The answer to 'how do we ship this test in a week, not a month'."),
 ("Regression to the mean","Extreme measurements drift back toward average on their own, with no intervention needed.","Whenever you target the worst-performing cohort and then claim credit for the rebound."),
 ("Base rate","How common the thing is before you see any evidence.","The quantity everyone drops in a Bayes question. A 99%-accurate test for a 1-in-10,000 disease mostly returns false positives."),
 ("Conditional independence","Two things stop being related once you know a third.","The assumption underneath Naive Bayes, and the thing a DAG question is really testing."),
 ("Collider bias","Conditioning on a shared consequence invents a correlation that was never there.","Selection-effect questions — 'we only have data on users who signed up' is usually a collider."),
 ("Berkson's paradox","Collider bias in a selected sample: two unrelated traits look negatively correlated inside the group.","Hospital-admission and hiring-pipeline examples in causal questions."),
 ("Backdoor criterion","The rule that tells you which variables to control for to get a causal estimate out of observational data.","Any 'can you prove causation without an experiment' question."),
 ("Bad controls","Variables that destroy your estimate when you adjust for them — usually things caused by the treatment.","The follow-up to 'just control for everything'. Controlling for a mediator throws away the effect you wanted."),
 ("Multicollinearity","Predictors so correlated that the model cannot tell their effects apart.","Unstable, sign-flipping coefficients in a linear model. Prediction is fine; interpretation is not."),
 ("VIF","Variance Inflation Factor — a number per predictor saying how much multicollinearity inflates its error bar.","The diagnostic you name after saying 'multicollinearity'. Above about 10 is the usual flag."),
 ("Power analysis","The calculation that turns a target effect size into a required sample size.","Before any experiment. It is how you answer 'how long do we run this?' with a number."),
 ("Position bias","People click the top result because it is on top, not because it is better.","Any ranking or recommender evaluation built on click logs."),
 ("Interleaving","Mixing two rankers' results in one list and seeing which gets the clicks.","A far more sensitive ranking comparison than a standard A/B, using a fraction of the traffic."),
]
